= Related Work <sec:related>

*Enforcement makes a cartel, and it is tied to the partner.* Without a binding contract, collusion survives only if a defection is expected to be punished @friedman1971 @greenporter1984. #cite(<calvano2020>, form: "prose") showed that Q-learning pricing algorithms learn such punishments on their own: forced to cut to the static best response for one period, they punish for a few rounds and then return gradually to the high price. On the path of play, LLM pricing agents answer a rival's cuts in the same reward-and-punishment pattern @fish2024. But a learned punishment answers a particular rival. Collusive Q-learning policies fall to Nash play on average against an unseen rival with identical parameters @eschenbaum2022. Among LLM agents, giving one seller less access to its rival's price history shrinks the markup, which the authors' model attributes to the less-informed seller being unable to detect a deviation and so to threaten punishment @keppo2026. Models from the same family also coordinate better than mixed ones in pure coordination games @aharon2026, so a successor built on its predecessor's model is the case most likely to keep a cartel going. No study has replaced one member of a working LLM cartel and asked whether its punishment survives.

*An LLM agent's strategy lives in its context.* Unlike a Q-learner, an LLM seller stores no learned strategy: each round a fixed model reads its prompt, the price history and the notes it wrote to itself. The notes matter. In a monopoly ablation by #cite(<fish2024>, form: "prose"), agents whose plans and insights were erased every round reached the monopoly price in 6 of 12 runs, against 12 of 12 with them. Fish et al. also rewrite the notes directly. Replacing a seller's plans early in play with one sentence warning against price wars raises its next price by about 5% of the markup. Our notes arms are the succession version of this "implantation", and they differ on four counts: a new seller rather than the same one, a real predecessor's full notes rather than one planted sentence, a cartel that has held for 150 rounds, and the response to a forced cut rather than the price level.

*High prices without enforcement.* The cartel price and the punishment that sustains it can come apart. In Calvano's market, Q-learning agents that keep the same partner raise prices even when the rival's price is hidden and a cut cannot be punished, so a test that looks only for punishment would miss the rise @arslan2026. Because antitrust law targets agreement, which software setting prices on its own may never reach, #cite(<harrington2018>, form: "prose") proposes making liability depend on whether a pricing algorithm embodies reward and punishment, tested in simulation, and recent audits read that structure from firms' frozen pricing policies @eschenbaum2026. An agent's stated reasoning is no shortcut: in LLM pricing agents, chain-of-thought faithfulness and collusion dissociate @leepark2026. And for an LLM seller, part of the policy is not frozen at all. It sits in notes that the seller rewrites every round and that a successor can inherit.

*What our design adds.* Prior work separates the cartel price from its enforcement by changing the environment (whether partners persist, whether rivals' prices are visible), and it studies LLM notes by editing them inside a seller that stays in place @arslan2026 @fish2024. We start from a cartel that already exists, replace one seller while keeping the market at two firms, and vary what the successor inherits: the price history, the predecessor's notes, both or neither, mainly with the same model. A forced cut then measures separately whether the successor keeps the cartel price and whether it punishes a defection. This points to which inherited material carries each, which is what a rule on inherited data would need to know (@sec:intro).
